{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-86294be4f471a0ef", "type": "tool_result", "content": "# Sylber 2.0: A Universal Syllable Embedding\narXiv: 2601.22306\nAuthors: Cheol Jun Cho, Nicholas Lee, Alan W Black, Gopala K. Anumanchipalli\nYear: 2026 | Venue: \nTopic: codec_vae\nStatus: unread\n\nContributions:\n  1. Sylber 2.0 learns a universal syllabification of speech that can detect syllables across many different languages without textual supervision.\n  2. Sylber 2.0 compresses speech to an average of 4.8 Hz across 102 languages, achieving the lowest token frequency ever reported for multilingual speech.\n  3. Sylber 2.0 significantly outperforms the original Sylber in reconstruction quality and approximates the performance of high-frequency tokens, even for expressive singing voice.\n  4. A zero-shot multispeaker TTS model trained with Sylber 2.0 achieves performance comparable to state-of-the-art models while using only 72M parameters.\n  5. The entire Sylber 2.0 framework requires minimal computational resources, fitting its training entirely on a single 24 GB memory GPU.\n\nTags: 压缩, 压缩/语义声学-语义, 压缩/语义声学-声学, TTS", "is_error": false}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "a8e80990-9f75-43e6-bbad-9b93e69ee810", "uuid": "3fa19010-3a18-4297-8deb-db98cd5c18cf", "timestamp": "2026-08-13T08:45:22.287Z", "tool_use_result": {"stdout": "# Sylber 2.0: A Universal Syllable Embedding\narXiv: 2601.22306\nAuthors: Cheol Jun Cho, Nicholas Lee, Alan W Black, Gopala K. Anumanchipalli\nYear: 2026 | Venue: \nTopic: codec_vae\nStatus: unread\n\nContributions:\n  1. Sylber 2.0 learns a universal syllabification of speech that can detect syllables across many different languages without textual supervision.\n  2. Sylber 2.0 compresses speech to an average of 4.8 Hz across 102 languages, achieving the lowest token frequency ever reported for multilingual speech.\n  3. Sylber 2.0 significantly outperforms the original Sylber in reconstruction quality and approximates the performance of high-frequency tokens, even for expressive singing voice.\n  4. A zero-shot multispeaker TTS model trained with Sylber 2.0 achieves performance comparable to state-of-the-art models while using only 72M parameters.\n  5. The entire Sylber 2.0 framework requires minimal computational resources, fitting its training entirely on a single 24 GB memory GPU.\n\nTags: 压缩, 压缩/语义声学-语义, 压缩/语义声学-声学, TTS", "stderr": "", "interrupted": false, "isImage": false, "noOutputExpected": false}}
{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-9dd67a140cb42cea", "type": "tool_result", "content": "# Make-A-Voice: Unified Voice Synthesis With Discrete Representation\narXiv: 2305.19269\nAuthors: Rongjie Huang, Chunlei Zhang, Yongqi Wang, Dongchao Yang, Luping Liu, Zhenhui Ye, Ziyue Jiang, Chao Weng, Zhou Zhao, Dong Yu\nYear: 2023 | Venue: \nTopic: tts_vc\nStatus: unread\n\nContributions:\n  1. We propose a unified voice synthesis framework called Make-A-Voice that utilizes a three-stage \"coarse-to-fine\" approach to model semantic meanings, acoustic conditions, and perceptual fidelity.\n  2. We enhance data scalability by leveraging self-supervised audio-only data for both the acoustic and generation stages without requiring annotations.\n  3. We achieve state-of-the-art results in style similarity and perceptual quality across three voice synthesis applications, demonstrating the framework's controllability and conditioning flexibility.\n\nTags: TTS, TTS/架构-AR-LLM, TTS/控制-参考语音, TTS/功能-多任务统一, TTS/功能-多说话人", "is_error": false}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "a8e80990-9f75-43e6-bbad-9b93e69ee810", "uuid": "a208ec87-7612-4009-8c67-2f6c6de6ed4f", "timestamp": "2026-08-13T08:45:22.571Z", "tool_use_result": {"stdout": "# Make-A-Voice: Unified Voice Synthesis With Discrete Representation\narXiv: 2305.19269\nAuthors: Rongjie Huang, Chunlei Zhang, Yongqi Wang, Dongchao Yang, Luping Liu, Zhenhui Ye, Ziyue Jiang, Chao Weng, Zhou Zhao, Dong Yu\nYear: 2023 | Venue: \nTopic: tts_vc\nStatus: unread\n\nContributions:\n  1. We propose a unified voice synthesis framework called Make-A-Voice that utilizes a three-stage \"coarse-to-fine\" approach to model semantic meanings, acoustic conditions, and perceptual fidelity.\n  2. We enhance data scalability by leveraging self-supervised audio-only data for both the acoustic and generation stages without requiring annotations.\n  3. We achieve state-of-the-art results in style similarity and perceptual quality across three voice synthesis applications, demonstrating the framework's controllability and conditioning flexibility.\n\nTags: TTS, TTS/架构-AR-LLM, TTS/控制-参考语音, TTS/功能-多任务统一, TTS/功能-多说话人", "stderr": "", "interrupted": false, "isImage": false, "noOutputExpected": false}}
