{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-832f15f576658d6a", "type": "tool_result", "content": "=== ABSTRACT ===\nAbstract Download PDF Abstract 1 Introduction 2 Related Work Text-to-Speech Synthesis. Singing Voice Synthesis. Unified Speech and Singing. Flow Matching and DiT. 3 Method 3.1 Preliminaries: Conditional Flow Matching 3.2 Architecture Model Configuration. Feature Extraction and Fusion. Adaptive Modulation. 3.3 Factorized Conditioning Why factorize? Content Conditioning. Melody Conditioning. Timbre Conditioning via In-Context Learning. Task Token. 3.4 Theoretical Analysis of Null-Token Marginalization The core problem. Connection to classifier-free guidance. 3.5 Training Strategy Data. Training Procedure. Inference. 4 UniSinging-Eval Benchmark Design Principles. Dataset Construction. 5 Experiments 5.1 Experimental Setup Baselines. Evaluation Protocol. Metrics. 5.2 Main Results Speech Generation. Singing Voice Generation. Data and Parameter Efficiency. 5.3 Ablation Studies Factorized conditioning is critical. Task tokens improve modality adaptation. Learned null tokens outperform fixed zeros. Melody conditioning is essential for singing. 6 Broader Impact and Limitations 7 Conclusion References A Extended Proofs and Theoretical Details A.1 Representation Conflict: Formal Definition A.2 Full Proof of Proposition A.3 Full Proof of Proposition (Null-Token Optimality) A.4 Null Token Training Dynamics A.5 Information-Theoretic Bound on Joint Training B Architecture Details DiT Configuration. C UniSinging-Eval Dataset Details License: CC BY 4.0 arXiv:2606.05852v1 [cs.SD] 04 Jun 2026 UniVoice: A Unified Model for Speech and Singing Voice Generation Junjie Zheng Affiliation: Giant Network Huixin Xue Affiliation: Shanghai Conservatory of Music Shihong Ren Affiliation: Shanghai Conservatory of Music Chaofan Ding Affiliation: Giant Network Hao Liu Affiliation: Shanghai Conservatory of Music Zihao Chen Affiliation: Giant Network Abstract Text-to-speech (TTS) and singing voice synthesis (SVS) both aim to generate human vocal audio from symbolic inputs, but they impose different requ\n\n=== METHOD ===\nFactorized Conditioning Why factorize? Content Conditioning. Melody Conditioning. Timbre Conditioning via In-Context Learning. Task Token. 3.4 Theoretical Analysis of Null-Token Marginalization The core problem. Connection to classifier-free guidance. 3.5 Training Strategy Data. Training Procedure. Inference. 4 UniSinging-Eval Benchmark Design Principles. Dataset Construction. 5 Experiments 5.1 Experimental Setup Baselines. Evaluation Protocol. Metrics. 5.2 Main Results Speech Generation. Singing Voice Generation. Data and Parameter Efficiency. 5.3 Ablation Studies Factorized conditioning is critical. Task tokens improve modality adaptation. Learned null tokens outperform fixed zeros. Melody conditioning is essential for singing. 6 Broader Impact and Limitations 7 Conclusion References A Extended Proofs and Theoretical Details A.1 Representation Conflict: Formal Definition A.2 Full Proof of Proposition A.3 Full Proof of Proposition (Null-Token Optimality) A.4 Null Token Training Dynamics A.5 Information-Theoretic Bound on Joint Training B Architecture Details DiT Configuration. C UniSinging-Eval Dataset Details License: CC BY 4.0 arXiv:2606.05852v1 [cs.SD] 04 Jun 2026 UniVoice: A Unified Model for Speech and Singing Voice Generation Junjie Zheng Affiliation: Giant Network Huixin Xue Affiliation: Shanghai Conservatory of Music Shihong Ren Affiliation: Shanghai Conservatory of Music Chaofan Ding Affiliation: Giant Network Hao Liu Affiliation: Shanghai Conservatory of Music Zihao Chen Affiliation: Giant Network Abstract Text-to-speech (TTS) and singing voice synthesis (SVS) both aim to generate human vocal audio from symbolic inputs, but they impose different requirements on the generation process. Speech generation relies on flexible, language-driven prosody, whereas singing generation requires explicit melody control and accurate rhythmic alignment. This mismatch makes it challenging to train a single model that can generate both natural speech and controllable singi", "is_error": false}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "a8e80990-9f75-43e6-bbad-9b93e69ee810", "uuid": "5b18ee47-db78-4d80-a23e-09eb668133f1", "timestamp": "2026-08-13T08:55:11.964Z", "tool_use_result": {"stdout": "=== ABSTRACT ===\nAbstract Download PDF Abstract 1 Introduction 2 Related Work Text-to-Speech Synthesis. Singing Voice Synthesis. Unified Speech and Singing. Flow Matching and DiT. 3 Method 3.1 Preliminaries: Conditional Flow Matching 3.2 Architecture Model Configuration. Feature Extraction and Fusion. Adaptive Modulation. 3.3 Factorized Conditioning Why factorize? Content Conditioning. Melody Conditioning. Timbre Conditioning via In-Context Learning. Task Token. 3.4 Theoretical Analysis of Null-Token Marginalization The core problem. Connection to classifier-free guidance. 3.5 Training Strategy Data. Training Procedure. Inference. 4 UniSinging-Eval Benchmark Design Principles. Dataset Construction. 5 Experiments 5.1 Experimental Setup Baselines. Evaluation Protocol. Metrics. 5.2 Main Results Speech Generation. Singing Voice Generation. Data and Parameter Efficiency. 5.3 Ablation Studies Factorized conditioning is critical. Task tokens improve modality adaptation. Learned null tokens outperform fixed zeros. Melody conditioning is essential for singing. 6 Broader Impact and Limitations 7 Conclusion References A Extended Proofs and Theoretical Details A.1 Representation Conflict: Formal Definition A.2 Full Proof of Proposition A.3 Full Proof of Proposition (Null-Token Optimality) A.4 Null Token Training Dynamics A.5 Information-Theoretic Bound on Joint Training B Architecture Details DiT Configuration. C UniSinging-Eval Dataset Details License: CC BY 4.0 arXiv:2606.05852v1 [cs.SD] 04 Jun 2026 UniVoice: A Unified Model for Speech and Singing Voice Generation Junjie Zheng Affiliation: Giant Network Huixin Xue Affiliation: Shanghai Conservatory of Music Shihong Ren Affiliation: Shanghai Conservatory of Music Chaofan Ding Affiliation: Giant Network Hao Liu Affiliation: Shanghai Conservatory of Music Zihao Chen Affiliation: Giant Network Abstract Text-to-speech (TTS) and singing voice synthesis (SVS) both aim to generate human vocal audio from symbolic inputs, but they impose different requ\n\n=== METHOD ===\nFactorized Conditioning Why factorize? Content Conditioning. Melody Conditioning. Timbre Conditioning via In-Context Learning. Task Token. 3.4 Theoretical Analysis of Null-Token Marginalization The core problem. Connection to classifier-free guidance. 3.5 Training Strategy Data. Training Procedure. Inference. 4 UniSinging-Eval Benchmark Design Principles. Dataset Construction. 5 Experiments 5.1 Experimental Setup Baselines. Evaluation Protocol. Metrics. 5.2 Main Results Speech Generation. Singing Voice Generation. Data and Parameter Efficiency. 5.3 Ablation Studies Factorized conditioning is critical. Task tokens improve modality adaptation. Learned null tokens outperform fixed zeros. Melody conditioning is essential for singing. 6 Broader Impact and Limitations 7 Conclusion References A Extended Proofs and Theoretical Details A.1 Representation Conflict: Formal Definition A.2 Full Proof of Proposition A.3 Full Proof of Proposition (Null-Token Optimality) A.4 Null Token Training Dynamics A.5 Information-Theoretic Bound on Joint Training B Architecture Details DiT Configuration. C UniSinging-Eval Dataset Details License: CC BY 4.0 arXiv:2606.05852v1 [cs.SD] 04 Jun 2026 UniVoice: A Unified Model for Speech and Singing Voice Generation Junjie Zheng Affiliation: Giant Network Huixin Xue Affiliation: Shanghai Conservatory of Music Shihong Ren Affiliation: Shanghai Conservatory of Music Chaofan Ding Affiliation: Giant Network Hao Liu Affiliation: Shanghai Conservatory of Music Zihao Chen Affiliation: Giant Network Abstract Text-to-speech (TTS) and singing voice synthesis (SVS) both aim to generate human vocal audio from symbolic inputs, but they impose different requirements on the generation process. Speech generation relies on flexible, language-driven prosody, whereas singing generation requires explicit melody control and accurate rhythmic alignment. This mismatch makes it challenging to train a single model that can generate both natural speech and controllable singi", "stderr": "", "interrupted": false, "isImage": false, "noOutputExpected": false}}
{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-927e18e218150287", "type": "tool_result", "content": "ransformer (DiT) backbone [ 10 ] , adapted from scalable video generation architectures [ 11 ] . Speech and singing are treated as two conditional modes of the same continuous flow, and a lightweight task token modulates the shared backbone without introducing separate task-specific heads. We further show that the learned null melody token can be interpreted as marginalizing over the melody variable for speech generation, providing a principled way to avoid imposing singing-specific controls on speech. We train UniVoice on 30k hours of speech and 35k hours of singing data. With 0.3B parameters, UniVoice achieves a speech PER of 5.26%, comparable to dedicated TTS systems such as F5-TTS (5.21%). On singing generation, it substantially outperforms the unified baseline Vevo2, reducing PER from 24.72% to 16.22%. Ablation studies show that factorized conditioning is essential for both modalities, and representation analyses indicate that the shared backbone preserves modality-specific structure while maintaining a common vocal generation space. To facilitate reproducible research, we will release the inference code, model checkpoints, and the UniSinging-Eval test set. Our contributions are summarized as follows: 1. We introduce a unified flow-matching architecture for speech and singing voice generation, using a single DiT backbone without separate task-specific heads. 2. We propose a factorized conditioning scheme that separates content, melody, and timbre, reducing the conflict between speech prosody modeling and singing melody control. 3. We introduce learned null melody conditioning for speech and analyze its connection to melody marginalization in conditional flow matching. 4. We present UniSinging-Eval , a benchmark for evaluating unified voice generation across 12 musical styles with phrase-level timestamps and style metadata. Figure 1 : Comparison of speech and singing voice generation paradigms. Speech synthesis (TTS) infers prosody implicitly from text, while singing voice synthesis (SVS) requires explicit melody control through MIDI notes. Naïvely combining the two leads to conflicting conditioning semantics. UniVoice resolves this conflict through factoriz", "is_error": false}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "a8e80990-9f75-43e6-bbad-9b93e69ee810", "uuid": "3e8a8da0-8b56-439b-8a96-008ade37a4bd", "timestamp": "2026-08-13T08:55:12.079Z", "tool_use_result": {"stdout": "ransformer (DiT) backbone [ 10 ] , adapted from scalable video generation architectures [ 11 ] . Speech and singing are treated as two conditional modes of the same continuous flow, and a lightweight task token modulates the shared backbone without introducing separate task-specific heads. We further show that the learned null melody token can be interpreted as marginalizing over the melody variable for speech generation, providing a principled way to avoid imposing singing-specific controls on speech. We train UniVoice on 30k hours of speech and 35k hours of singing data. With 0.3B parameters, UniVoice achieves a speech PER of 5.26%, comparable to dedicated TTS systems such as F5-TTS (5.21%). On singing generation, it substantially outperforms the unified baseline Vevo2, reducing PER from 24.72% to 16.22%. Ablation studies show that factorized conditioning is essential for both modalities, and representation analyses indicate that the shared backbone preserves modality-specific structure while maintaining a common vocal generation space. To facilitate reproducible research, we will release the inference code, model checkpoints, and the UniSinging-Eval test set. Our contributions are summarized as follows: 1. We introduce a unified flow-matching architecture for speech and singing voice generation, using a single DiT backbone without separate task-specific heads. 2. We propose a factorized conditioning scheme that separates content, melody, and timbre, reducing the conflict between speech prosody modeling and singing melody control. 3. We introduce learned null melody conditioning for speech and analyze its connection to melody marginalization in conditional flow matching. 4. We present UniSinging-Eval , a benchmark for evaluating unified voice generation across 12 musical styles with phrase-level timestamps and style metadata. Figure 1 : Comparison of speech and singing voice generation paradigms. Speech synthesis (TTS) infers prosody implicitly from text, while singing voice synthesis (SVS) requires explicit melody control through MIDI notes. Naïvely combining the two leads to conflicting conditioning semantics. UniVoice resolves this conflict through factoriz", "stderr": "", "interrupted": false, "isImage": false, "noOutputExpected": false}}
